As a point & click adaptation, it loses something of the pacing, intensity, and imaginative power of Buchan’s rollercoaster, only really picking up towards the very end despite a 4 to 5 hour running time. Again, against more traditional video game experiences – even the likes of Dear Esther – it struggles to satisfy when players actions have comparatively little effect.
But it still contains many memorable encounters and if The 39 Steps can convince players to check out the original story (free from
